.TH BHIST-SPEEDUP 1 "September 11, 2026" "Volclava 2.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bhist, bhist-speedup, bhist-speedup-server, bhist-speedup-loader
\- accelerated retained job history
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bhist
.RI [ bhist-options ]
.RI [ job_ID ]
.br
.B bhist-speedup-loader
.BR start | rebuild | stop | status
.br
.B bhist-speedup-server
.BR start | stop | status | view
.br
.B bhist-speedup-server
.B perfmon
.BR seconds | stop
.br
.B $LSF_ENVDIR/bhist-speedup
.BR start | stop | status | restart | rebuild
.br
.B /etc/init.d/bhist-speedup
.BR start | stop | status | restart | rebuild
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bhist
is the public entry point.  Supported requests are sent to the accelerated
client; unsupported requests execute
.B bhist_original
directly.  The server selects retained events from SQLite and passes them to
.B bhist_original -f
for native replay and formatting.
.PP
The accelerated subset includes
.BR -l ,
.BR -w ,
.BR -a ,
.BR -d ,
.BR -e ,
.BR -p ,
.BR -s ,
.BR -r ,
.BR -u ,
.BR -q ,
.BR -m ,
.BR -S ,
.BR -D ,
.BR -C ,
and zero or one ordinary or array job selector.  A time-range query does not
require a job identifier.  Multiple job selectors and all other options are
handled by native bhist.
.PP
.B -h
and
.B -V
always execute native bhist.  The wrapper also understands attached option
values, combined short options, and
.BR -- .
.PP
The wrapper checks the operating system before loading the accelerated client.
On CentOS, it inspects the cluster markers
.I $LSF_ENVDIR/lsf.entitlement
and
.IR $LSF_ENVDIR/profile.lsf .
An existing marker identifies LSF; a dangling marker link or an invalid or
inaccessible environment directory leaves the type unknown.  Both cases
execute
.B bhist_original
directly, with the usual fallback message.  LSF_ENVDIR must be an absolute,
readable, searchable directory before the absence of both markers is treated
as Volclava and acceleration is attempted.
.PP
These checks happen before the accelerated executable is loaded, so a missing
client shared library cannot prevent an environment-based native route.
Neither the operating-system file nor the markers are executed, marker
contents are not read, and the wrapper does not invoke
.BR lsid .
The shell check treats failure to read or search the directory as unknown;
it does not expose a separate result for every possible filesystem error.
.PP
On other or unidentified operating systems, supported requests continue to
the accelerated client.  Only the wrapper performs environment routing.
The C configuration code does not collect operating-system or cluster-type
fields, and the accelerated client does not apply a platform fallback.
.PP
The
.B ID
field in
.I /etc/os-release
is authoritative.  A compatibility
.I /etc/centos-release
file is consulted only when os-release is unavailable or contains no ID, so
Rocky Linux is not treated as CentOS.
.SH FALLBACK
The wrapper does not probe the service before a query.  If configuration,
connection, transport, or query processing fails before accelerated standard
output is emitted, it reports the failure and executes
.B bhist_original
once.  After accelerated standard output has begun, a failure is reported as
an incomplete result and native bhist is not executed, preventing duplicated
output.  A no-match result is successful acceleration and does not fall back.
.PP
Direct invocation of
.B bhist-speedup
always enters the accelerated query path, subject to its usual configuration
and request checks.  It returns 125 for a failure that permits safe native
fallback before query output, but does not execute native bhist itself.
Only the wrapper performs that fallback.  Errors after accelerated standard
output has begun return 126.  Other exit statuses, including an ELF loader's
127, do not trigger a general fallback.  Direct client invocation still
requires its runtime shared libraries to load successfully.
.SH LOADER
.TP
.B bhist-speedup-loader start
Start or resume loading.  A database marked
.B rebuild_required
is not resumed.
.TP
.B bhist-speedup-loader rebuild
Build and atomically publish a new schema-version-3 database.  This command
fails while the loader is already running.
.TP
.B bhist-speedup-loader stop
Stop the loader.
.TP
.B bhist-speedup-loader status
Print process state, database and event directory paths, database health,
and tracked event rows.
.PP
The loader samples the current
.B lsb.events
size once per second.  Truncation persists
.B db_status=rebuild_required
and stops live import before the next iteration.
.SH SERVER
.TP
.B bhist-speedup-server start
Start the query server. Database existence is not a startup dependency.
.TP
.B bhist-speedup-server stop
Stop the server and active query process groups.
.TP
.B bhist-speedup-server status
Print process state and current request load.
.TP
.B bhist-speedup-server view
Print the latest completed performance sample period and exit.
.TP
.BI "bhist-speedup-server perfmon " seconds
Start fixed, non-overlapping in-memory sample periods.
.TP
.B bhist-speedup-server perfmon stop
Stop sampling and discard performance data.
.SH SERVICE ADMINISTRATION
Operational server and loader commands require root or a configured cluster
administrator and must run on the host
named by
.BR LSB_BHIST_ENDPOINT .
The original real user ID determines the caller; USER and LOGNAME do not grant
administrator access.  Real and effective user IDs must match.
Local help and version output do not require configuration or administrator
privileges.
.PP
Every management command first loads the service configuration and the
administrator list, then checks the caller and service host.  Root keeps its
identity for the stop and status commands, allowing it to inspect and stop
instances owned by another administrator or by root.
.PP
Before dispatching start, rebuild, view, or either form of perfmon, root
switches to the first administrator in the expanded, deduplicated list.
This includes
.BR "perfmon stop" .
The selected account must resolve to a nonzero user ID; root does not skip an
invalid first account and select a later administrator.  Stop and status do not
select a target user ID, but still require valid configuration and all caller
and service-host checks.
.PP
Any non-root user in the administrator list keeps their own identity, even
when they are not the first administrator.  No user switch is attempted for
that caller.  Passing the administrator check does not grant access to another
user's files or permission to stop another user's process.  A root invocation
of start, rebuild, view, or perfmon has the first administrator's permissions
after switching identity.
.PP
Configuration, administrator lookup, host-check, or identity-switch failures
terminate the affected command before the requested operation.  Stop and status
do not bypass damaged administrator configuration; repair the configuration
before retrying.  The program does not change existing file ownership
automatically.  A root status query can cause SQLite to recreate WAL/SHM
sidecar files; their ownership follows the existing database owner, so the
query does not leave root-owned runtime files that block later administrator
use.
.SS Upgrading existing root-owned services
Keep the original service configuration until existing instances have stopped.
Use the new stop command as root, or use the old commands, and confirm that
the main and worker processes have exited before changing configuration and
preparing files for the next runtime user.  The new root stop command can stop
an old root-owned process, but still requires valid service and administrator
configuration on the configured service host.
.PP
Choose the administrator that will run each service and prepare only the
speedup component's own database directory, database and sidecar files, logs,
and PID files for that user.  Do not recursively change the ownership of the
shared LSF_LOGDIR, or change ownership of lsb.events and its archives.  The
runtime user needs access to the configuration and event files.  Reuse the
existing database after migration; this identity change does not require a
rebuild.
.PP
Root selects the current first administrator for start, rebuild, view, and
perfmon.  Root stop and status retain root.  Stop the existing instance before
changing administrator order, removing its account, or choosing a different
user to start it.  Existing processes and file owners do not change when the
administrator list changes, and stopping a service does not migrate its files.
.SS Unified service commands
When bhist speedup support is enabled at build time, the installation includes
.IR $LSF_ENVDIR/bhist-speedup .
This Bash script manages both components by invoking their existing management
commands.  Use its full path to distinguish it from the accelerated query
client named
.IR $LSF_BINDIR/bhist-speedup .
.PP
The script loads the readable shell profile from
.I /etc/profile.d/volclava.sh
or
.IR /etc/profile.d/lsf.sh .
Only one of these profiles is expected to exist.  It clears inherited
LSF_ENVDIR and LSF_BINDIR before loading the profile, then checks that the
resulting directories and both management executables are available.
This supplies the environment even when no login shell has run.
.TP
.B start
Run loader start, then server start.  A loader startup failure prevents the
server startup command.
.TP
.B stop
Run server stop, then loader stop.  Both stops are attempted even if one
fails; the script reports failure if either command fails.
.TP
.B status
Print both server and loader status, with component labels.
.TP
.B restart
Stop both components, then run loader start and server start.
This is an ordinary restart that reuses the existing database.
.TP
.B rebuild
Stop both components, then run loader rebuild and server start.
The loader removes the existing database and rebuilds it from retained event
files; the script does not delete database files itself.
.PP
Restart and rebuild proceed only after both stop commands succeed.  A loader
start or rebuild failure prevents server startup.  The underlying programs
start their daemons in the background; a successful startup command does not
mean that the database has finished loading.  Use loader status to check
database readiness.  No automatic retry or rollback is performed.
.PP
The script preserves the root and cluster-administrator policy described
above.  Its commands return 0 on success, 1 on operation failure, or 2 for
invalid arguments.
.SS Systemd service
The post stage of
.B volcinstall.sh
registers bhist speedup when the component is installed and systemd is
available.  It copies the management script to
.I /etc/init.d/bhist-speedup
and writes a native unit at
.IR /etc/systemd/system/bhist-speedup.service .
Registration reloads systemd's unit definitions but does not start the service
or enable startup at boot.  Repeating post refreshes the files without
restarting the service or changing its enabled state.  An unavailable systemd
causes registration to be skipped; a registration failure is reported without
aborting the software installation.  Direct RPM or DEB installation does not
register this bhist service; registration is performed by the installer post
stage.
.PP
On the configured service host, an operator with the host's existing systemd
management permissions can run:
.PP
.nf
systemctl start bhist-speedup
systemctl stop bhist-speedup
systemctl restart bhist-speedup
systemctl enable bhist-speedup
.fi
.PP
The unit maps ExecStart and ExecStop to the script's start and stop commands.
Systemctl restart is an ordinary stop followed by start; it does not rebuild
the database.  To request a rebuild, invoke
.B /etc/init.d/bhist-speedup rebuild
directly.  No reload or rebuild action is added to systemd, and no polkit or
sudoers rules are installed.
.PP
The unit uses
.B Type=oneshot
and
.BR RemainAfterExit=yes .
After the launcher returns successfully, systemd normally shows
.BR "active (exited)" .
This describes the launcher state, not daemon health or database readiness.
The unit does not automatically restart failed daemons.  It starts its
management commands as root, and the binaries apply their existing
administrator identity-switching rules.
.PP
Direct script commands do not synchronize the unit's state, and daemons
started directly are not automatically placed in the unit's control group.
Use the script's status and stop commands to inspect and stop directly
managed instances.
.PP
.B volcuninstall.sh
stops and disables the registered service, removes the copied script and
unit, and reloads systemd before removing the software.  Missing registration
files are harmless and repeated cleanup is supported.  Registration cleanup
preserves database files, source events, and logs.
.SH CONFIGURATION
All speedup components read:
.PP
.I $LSF_ENVDIR/lsb.hist
.PP
The file contains literal
.I KEY=VALUE
assignments. Empty lines, comments, surrounding whitespace, and one pair of
whole-value quotes are accepted. Unknown and duplicate keys are errors.
.PP
.B LSB_BHIST_ENDPOINT
must be set to
.IR hostname:port .
The server listens on all local IPv4 addresses (0.0.0.0) at the specified port.
The hostname is used for client connections and service-host validation;
it does not select the server's listening interface.
The generated template provides only a commented example.  The database path
must be absolute and should use local SSD storage.
.B LSB_BHIST_EVENT_DIR
must name the absolute directory containing
.B lsb.events
and rotated archives; its generated example is also commented out.
.PP
Cluster-owned settings are inherited from
.IR $LSF_ENVDIR/lsf.conf .
.B LSF_BINDIR
selects
.BR bhist_original ;
.B LSF_LOGDIR
and
.B LSF_LOG_MASK
control daemon logs.
.PP
.B LSF_CONFDIR
selects the cluster configuration directory independently of LSF_ENVDIR.
For every server or loader management command, the program reads the Cluster
table in
.IR $LSF_CONFDIR/lsf.shared ,
deduplicates ClusterName values, and requires exactly one corresponding
.I lsf.cluster.<name>
file to exist.  Administrators are read from its ClusterAdmins/Administrators
definition; the older ClusterManager/Manager form is accepted, with the modern
definition taking precedence.
.PP
Administrator items are resolved as user names first, then Unix group names.
Groups expand using their existing member list; duplicate user names are
removed while preserving first occurrence order.  When a command requires an
identity switch, root uses the first user in this resulting list, rather than a
hard-coded account named lsfadmin.  An empty or unresolvable administrator list
is an error for all management commands, including root stop and status.
.PP
The ordinary client does not read the administrator list or require service
management privileges.  Offline configuration and route detection work without
running cluster daemons; successful historical queries still require the
appropriate data and query service.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $LSF_BINDIR/bhist
Public POSIX wrapper.
.TP
.I $LSF_BINDIR/bhist-speedup
Internal accelerated client.
.TP
.I $LSF_BINDIR/bhist_original
Native bhist formatter and fallback command.
.TP
.I $LSF_ENVDIR/bhist-speedup
Unified loader and server management script.
.TP
.I /etc/init.d/bhist-speedup
Management script copied by the installer post stage.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/bhist-speedup.service
Native systemd unit registered by the installer post stage.
.TP
.I $LSF_ENVDIR/lsb.hist
Speedup configuration.
.TP
.I $LSF_CONFDIR/lsf.shared
Cluster names used to select the local cluster configuration.
.TP
.I $LSF_CONFDIR/lsf.cluster.<name>
Selected cluster administrator configuration.
.TP
.I directory-of-LSB_BHIST_DB_PATH/bhist-speedup-server.pid
Locked server PID file.
.TP
.I directory-of-LSB_BHIST_DB_PATH/bhist-speedup-loader.pid
Locked loader PID file.
.TP
.I $LSF_LOGDIR/bhist-speedup-server.log.HOST
Server log.
.TP
.I $LSF_LOGDIR/bhist-speedup-loader.log.HOST
Loader log.
.SH SECURITY
The TCP protocol is unauthenticated. The client-provided local username is a
default
.B -u
filter hint, not a verified identity. Restrict the endpoint to a trusted
cluster network.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bhist (1)
